      "body": "## Change Summary\n\n- **What changed?**\n  - Added `_release_loader_cached_memory()` to the standalone eval policy server and\n    call it once after `build_model_spec()`, before warmup. It runs\n    `torch.cuda.empty_cache()` and logs the resulting reserved-memory drop.\n  - Added unit tests covering the CUDA and non-CUDA paths.\n- **Which issue or requirement does this address?**\n  - Eval factories that build in fp32 and cast to bf16 (pi05, xvla, GR00T-N1.6/N1.7,\n    DreamZero) keep the transient fp32 copy and the staged checkpoint tensors in\n    PyTorch's caching allocator. For pi0.5 RoboTwin, `nvidia-smi` reported 15.9GB\n    after load while the live weights were 8.3GB, which reduced the headroom left\n    for co-located GPU simulators.\n  - Releasing those blocks drops the pi0.5 load footprint from 15.9GB to 8.7GB,\n    freeing ~7GB per card for co-located sims. At the measured ~5.5GB/sim for\n    RoboTwin, that is 1-2 more sims per card. Co-location still works without the\n    release, but with a lower packing limit and a higher OOM risk.\n- **Which modules are affected?**\n  - `loongforge/embodied/eval/servers/loongforge_server.py`\n  - `loongforge/embodied/eval/unit_tests/test_server_memory_release.py`\n  - Applies to every eval model factory; no model-specific code changed.\n\n## Validation\n\n- [x] I ran the relevant local checks.\n  - `pytest loongforge/embodied/eval/unit_tests` -> 146 passed, 1 skipped (full eval\n    suite, run on the branch stacked on `feat/eval-orchestration-parallel`).\n  - The two new tests also pass on the master-based branch.\n  - Local reference measurement on A800 (pi0.5 RoboTwin, bf16, not required for CI):\n    `nvidia-smi` 49,183 -> 65,057 MB after build, 57,877 MB after the release;\n    `memory_reserved` 15.02 -> 8.01 GiB; `memory_allocated` unchanged at 7.72 GiB.\n- [x] I added or updated tests where appropriate.\n  - 2 tests: `empty_cache` is called exactly once when CUDA is available; no-op when\n    CUDA is unavailable.\n- [x] I checked compatibility with existing APIs and configurations.\n  - No public API or config changes; no-op on CPU; only cached free blocks are\n    released, live parameters are untouched.\n\n## CI, GPU, and Image Impact\n\n- [ ] This change affects dependencies, Dockerfiles, patches, or runtime environments.\n- [ ] GPU validation is required. Suggested suite: `llm_vlm` / `embodied`.\n- [ ] A local candidate image should be built during GPU validation.\n- [x] No GPU or image validation is required.\n\n## Reviewer Notes\n\n- The call happens **before** warmup, so warmup activations repopulate the allocator\n  with inference-sized blocks. Releasing after warmup would free those too and force\n  a fresh `cudaMalloc` on the first real request.\n- `nvidia-smi` after load is ~8.7GB (7.72 GiB live weights + ~0.7 GiB CUDA\n  context/workspace), so `vram_model_gb` should be budgeted as ~10GB including\n  warmup activations.\n- No correctness or steady-state latency change; capacity only.\n- Follow-up (not in this PR): `PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True` for\n  long-running multi-process servers; weight tying for the duplicated 0.53B `lm_head`\n  in pi0.5 (~1GB bf16).",
